From 1bb8ffd919c6ed50b255551056bc9a15f85d5b59 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 11 Nov 2026 08:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] VideoPlayer: a film with soft telecine in an MPEG-2 file is
 shown at 23.976 Hz, each picture once

A film on a DVD, or from one, is MPEG-2 coded at 23.976 pictures per second
that carries the flags to be shown at 29.97 (repeat_first_field on every
second picture, top_field_first changing). Kodi took it for 29.97 video,
chose 59.94 Hz and showed each picture of the film 3 and 2 refreshes in turn.

Mpeg2SoftTelecine.h (no dependencies) reads the sequence extension and the
picture headers of a packet and decides from them, without decoding, whether
a stream has that cadence: the pictures of each group of pictures are put in
display order (temporal reference) and a window of 24 of them must be
progressive frames of a non-progressive sequence, with repeat_first_field on
every second one and the fields following each other top, bottom, top,
bottom across all of them. Real video, hard telecine, 29.97 progressive
without repeated fields, 2:2, and flags that do not follow the cadence are
not film. Film is left after 120 pictures without such a window.

CDVDDemuxFFmpeg::PrefetchForSoftTelecine() reads up to 600 packets / 4 MiB /
1.5 s ahead at Open() of a file (a plain input; not a disc, whose navigation
runs inside the reading, nor a live stream, nor a transport stream, nor a
library scan), keeps them and gives them again from ReadFrame() (cleared on
Flush, SeekTime and Dispose). If the stream is film, its demux stream says
24000/1001, not interlaced, softTelecine: the mode is chosen once at open,
23.976 Hz, and kodi.log says so. The detector decides in 25 to 35 pictures.

CVideoPlayerVideo shows the non-interlaced pictures of such a stream with
FilmTimer.h: each picture at the n-th of the times 1001/24000 s apart from the
first, restarting from the stream's time if it is more than 25 ms away, for
the normal speed only; the repeated field is not used. An interlaced picture
(credits in video) goes as before.

The pictures shown are the decoded ones; only when each is shown is made even.

Measured on the host:
- the detector on 25 files (soft telecine with and without B pictures, open
  and closed groups, a cadence break, in a VOB, an elementary stream and
  Matroska with the times of the disc; and not film: hard telecine, video,
  29.97 progressive, 2:2, every flag set, random flags, the repeated field
  alternating with the top field always first, the same flags in a
  progressive sequence, the same flags with progressive_frame cleared, 12
  pictures of cadence and then random flags): all as wanted; film is
  recognised after 25-35 pictures, in the first ~1 MB; 9 mutants of the header
  fail the checks. Film, then video, then film: film, not film after 134
  pictures of video, film again after 29 pictures of film.
- the read-ahead loop of the demuxer (same limits) on 17 files: the packets
  that come out, read-ahead ones first, are exactly those of a plain read.
- the timing path of VideoPlayerVideo replayed on the pictures of the
  demuxer (kodisim, with the PTSTracker of Hades unchanged): DVD, VOB, .m2v
  and Matroska with uniform times or with the 3:2 times of the disc, which
  step 0 did not fix, all give 1 refresh per picture at 23.976 Hz in 8 of 8
  phases (steps of 41.71 ms); the frame rate detector settles on 23.976.
  FilmTimer: 6 groups of checks, 5 mutants fail.

Not done: a disc (DVD, ISO, VIDEO_TS) gets no mode of its own: its
navigation events run inside the reading and a read ahead there would run
them outside the player's loop; a file that starts with video and has film
later is shown as before; nothing was seen on a screen.
